(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-2-pdm-exam-2-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"PDM I Exam 2 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which types of stress test are described?","choices":["Exercise, pharmacological, treadmill and bicycle","Nuclear, magnetic resonance, catheter and Doppler","Treadmill, bicycle, handgrip and cold pressor","Exercise, echocardiographic, nuclear, pharmacological"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Two axes run through the list: how the heart is stressed, and how it is then imaged, and the four names are combinations of those two. Echocardiographic, nuclear and pharmacological. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"The overall vector at a given moment is directed away from a lead. What does that lead record?","choices":["A negative deflection","A positive deflection","An isoelectric line","No tracing at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the converse holds: toward the lead gives a positive deflection, which is why the same beat looks different in different leads. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which is a stated indication for coronary computed tomography angiography?","choices":["Assessing myocardial viability after infarction","Evaluating an equivocal or non-diagnostic stress test","Measuring pulmonary artery occlusion pressure","Locating the origin of an arrhythmia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It answers the question the stress test left open, alongside assessing chest pain and detecting or excluding stenosis and plaque. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A man has a corrected QT interval of 480 milliseconds. How should that be read?","choices":["Normal for a man","High risk prolongation","Prolonged only in women","Borderline or prolonged"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Above 450 milliseconds in a man, or 460 in a woman, up to 500 milliseconds is borderline or prolonged, and only above 500 is it high risk. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"How is regularity determined when it is not obvious by eye?","choices":["By counting the number of P waves in six seconds","By comparing consecutive R to R intervals with calipers","By measuring the QT interval in several leads","By assessing the isoelectric line on the TP segment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The marked paper is the field substitute, and it answers the same question the calipers do. Or a marked sheet of paper. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What does LEFT heart catheterization assess?","choices":["Pulmonary artery pressure and vascular resistance","Coronary anatomy, aortic pressure and left ventricular function","Tricuspid and pulmonic valve function","Right atrial pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Everything on the systemic side of the circulation, which is the side the left heart serves. Together with systemic vascular resistance, and aortic and mitral valve function, and left ventricular pressure. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man with uremia bleeds easily. His platelet count, prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time are normal. Which category fits?","choices":["Factor VII deficiency","Platelet dysfunction","Hemophilia A","Hypofibrinogenemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Normal prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time with bleeding suggest platelet dysfunction; uremia is one cause, and platelet function testing is abnormal. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which fascicle of the left bundle branch extends across the anterior wall of the left ventricle?","choices":["The left posterior fascicle","The left anterior fascicle","The intraventricular septal fascicle","The right bundle branch"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Of the left fascicles it is the one that extends across the anterior wall of the left ventricle. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Where is the U wave best seen, and what is it thought to represent?","choices":["In lead II, representing atrial repolarization","In aVR, representing septal depolarization","In V2 and V3, possibly repolarization of the Purkinje fibers","In V5 and V6, representing delayed ventricular filling"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the hedging is honest: there is no clear understanding of what it represents, so the association with electrolytes is more useful than the theory. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What does Doppler add to an echocardiogram?","choices":["Myocardial tracer uptake","The coronary calcium burden","Blood flow and velocity","Electrical conduction pathways"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Doppler shows flow and velocity through the heart chambers and great vessels, which the grayscale picture of structure alone does not provide. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman has von Willebrand disease. Which phase of hemostasis does the missing factor serve?","choices":["Secondary hemostasis","Fibrinolysis","Primary hemostasis","Vasoconstriction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. von Willebrand factor tethers platelets to exposed subendothelial collagen, so its deficiency impairs formation of the platelet plug. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 49-year-old asks which lipoprotein is the principal driver of atherosclerotic disease. Which is it?","choices":["HDL (high-density lipoprotein)","Chylomicrons","Albumin","LDL (low-density lipoprotein)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. LDL is the major cholesterol carrier and the primary target for prevention; LDL cholesterol and cardiovascular risk have a log-linear relationship. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"For which patient is an echocardiographic stress test the appropriate initial choice?","choices":["One who cannot exercise at all","One with a normal resting electrocardiogram who can exercise","One with suspected aortic dissection","One who can exercise but whose resting tracing is abnormal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The electrocardiogram cannot be read for ischemia if it is already abnormal, so the imaging has to carry the answer instead. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 74-year-old has an N-terminal pro B-type natriuretic peptide drawn. Where does this molecule come from?","choices":["It is the inert fragment of cleaved pro-BNP","It is the active hormone made by the liver","It is released when myocytes become necrotic","It is a troponin subunit found in the atria"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cardiomyocytes secrete pro B-type natriuretic peptide in response to stretch, and it is cleaved into biologically active BNP (B-type natriuretic peptide) and inert NT-proBNP. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 58-year-old still has an elevated troponin four weeks after a myocardial infarction. What does a late elevation relate to?","choices":["Higher left ventricular ejection fraction","A falsely positive assay","Lower left ventricular ejection fraction","A reduced risk of reinfarction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Elevated late levels at 4 weeks are inversely related to left ventricular ejection fraction, which is how troponin estimates infarct size and impact. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 65-year-old had a stent placed yesterday and his chest pain has returned. Which use of troponin applies?","choices":["Guiding heart failure therapy","Screening for statin myopathy","Refining long-term risk","Detecting reinfarction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Troponins detect reocclusion and reinfarction, and procedure-related infarction, among their applications beyond the first diagnosis. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 5-year-old girl has lifelong severe bleeding of any tissue, and all screening clotting tests are normal. Which specific assay is next?","choices":["Factor VIII assay","Factor XIII assay","Mixing study","Fibrinogen antigen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When screens are normal and factor XIII deficiency is suspected, the next tests are a clot stability test and a factor XIII assay. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is done before a cardiovascular magnetic resonance study is ordered?","choices":["An echocardiogram","A coronary angiogram","A nuclear perfusion study","A coronary calcium score"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is the first-line functional study, and magnetic resonance answers what it leaves unresolved rather than replacing it. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"In which patients is cephalization seen?","choices":["Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","Coronary artery disease without failure","Heart failure and pulmonary hypertension","Pericardial calcification"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both raise pressure in the pulmonary circulation, and the redistribution upward is the radiographic consequence. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman has normal prothrombin time, partial thromboplastin time, fibrinogen and platelets, and a raised D-dimer. Which interpretation fits?","choices":["Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Hemophilia A","Platelet dysfunction","Acute thrombosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A raised D-dimer with normal coagulation studies and platelets is the acute thrombosis pattern, and D-dimer is nonspecific. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 15-year-old boy has a prolonged prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time with a normal thrombin time. Which deficiency fits?","choices":["Factor X, V or II","Factor VII only","Factor VIII only","Hypofibrinogenemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A prolonged prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time with a normal thrombin time points to deficiency of common pathway factors, factor X, V or II. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Why is transthoracic echocardiography the most common echo procedure?","choices":["It can be performed at the bedside and is noninvasive","It gives better detail of the posterior structures","It requires no operator training","It is the only echo that can use Doppler"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Availability rather than image quality is what makes it the default, and the trade is detail at the back of the heart. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with sepsis is evaluated for disseminated intravascular coagulation. Which finding is decreased?","choices":["Fibrin degradation products","Prothrombin time","Activated partial thromboplastin time","Protein C, antithrombin and protein S"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In disseminated intravascular coagulation the protease inhibitors, including protein C, antithrombin and protein S, are decreased. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman has a vessel injury, and a physician explains how the clot is stabilized. Which phase forms insoluble, cross-linked fibrin through thrombin?","choices":["Primary hemostasis","Fibrinolysis","Secondary hemostasis","Vasoconstriction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Secondary hemostasis is formation of insoluble, cross-linked fibrin by activated coagulation factors, specifically thrombin; the coagulation cascade drives it. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What is coronary angiography the gold standard for?","choices":["Assessing myocardial viability","Coronary artery anatomy and cardiac function","Measuring the coronary calcium score","Detecting valve vegetations"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is why it is usually performed before any percutaneous or surgical intervention on the coronary arteries. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 50-year-old has an elevated lipoprotein(a) and an LDL-C (low-density lipoprotein cholesterol) at goal on a statin. What does the elevated lipoprotein(a) prompt?","choices":["Doubling the statin to lower the lipoprotein(a)","Earlier, more intensive control of other risk factors","Repeating lipoprotein(a) every 3 months","No change, since it is not a risk factor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lipoprotein(a) is a largely fixed genetic risk factor measured once; elevation prompts earlier and more intensive management of every other modifiable risk factor, since statins do not lower it. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man bleeds despite a normal platelet count, platelet function, prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time. Which deficiency should be considered?","choices":["Hemophilia A","Factor VII deficiency","Factor XIII deficiency","Heparin effect"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Factor XIII or alpha-2 antiplasmin deficiency fits: normal prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time with bleeding suggest a platelet disorder, mild von Willebrand disease, factor XIII or alpha-2 antiplasmin deficiency, or impaired fibrinolysis. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Into how many fascicles does the left bundle branch divide, and what are they?","choices":["Two: the left anterior and the left posterior","Four, one to each wall of the ventricle","It does not divide; it terminates directly in Purkinje fibers","Three: left anterior, left posterior and septal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The septal fascicle is the one most often forgotten, which matters because the three-fascicle arrangement is what makes bifascicular block possible. The left anterior. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Where does the sinoatrial node sit?","choices":["In the upper wall of the right atrium","At the junction of the atria and the ventricles","Within the interventricular septum","In the wall of the left atrium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and being the highest pacemaker in the chain is what makes it the one that normally sets the rate. Just below the opening of the vena cava. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 42-year-old eats a fatty meal and chylomicrons appear in her plasma. Which apolipoprotein do they carry?","choices":["ApoB-100 (apolipoprotein B-100), made by the liver","ApoB-48 (apolipoprotein B-48), made by the intestine","ApoA-I (apolipoprotein A-I), made by the liver","Apo(a) (apolipoprotein(a)), made by the liver"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. ApoB-48 is synthesized by the intestines and found in chylomicrons, which carry dietary triglyceride. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 44-year-old with a strong family history has lipoprotein(a) measured. What is lipoprotein(a)?","choices":["An HDL-like particle with apo(a) bound to apoA-I","A chylomicron remnant that carries apoB-48","An LDL-like particle with apo(a) bound to apoB-100","A free apolipoprotein that circulates alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lipoprotein(a) is an LDL (low-density lipoprotein)-like particle in which apo(a) (apolipoprotein(a)) is bound to apoB-100 (apolipoprotein B-100). It is more atherogenic than LDL. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 53-year-old's report shows lipoprotein(a) and apoB (apolipoprotein B) on the same panel. Why are they reported together?","choices":["Lipoprotein(a) adds to the apoB count","ApoB is made from lipoprotein(a)","Both are acute-phase reactants","Both are fixed genetic values"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Each lipoprotein(a) particle carries one apoB-100 (apolipoprotein B-100), so it contributes to the total apoB particle count. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is obtained before an exercise stress test begins?","choices":["A resting echocardiogram in every case","A coronary calcium score","A resting electrocardiogram","A radionuclide injection at rest"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Without the resting baseline there is nothing to compare the exercise tracing against. Blood pressure both supine and standing. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is happening mechanically during the ST segment?","choices":["The ventricles are filling during diastole","The atria are contracting","The ventricles are repolarizing","The ventricles are holding their contraction to allow emptying"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It falls between ventricular depolarization and repolarization, and it is the part of the beat that does the ejecting. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which combination of advantages does vascular ultrasound offer?","choices":["Fast, portable, repeatable, no ionizing radiation","Superior resolution to computed tomography with no operator dependence","Reliable assessment of deep vessels regardless of body habitus","Independence from the angle at which the probe is held"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The combination of no radiation and easy repetition is what makes it the study you can do again tomorrow without cost to the patient. It shows both anatomy and flow. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 53-year-old man has a fibrinogen above the reference range of 2.0 to 4.0 grams per liter. What does an elevated result indicate?","choices":["Tissue damage or inflammation","A clotting factor deficiency","Vitamin K deficiency","Platelet dysfunction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Elevated fibrinogen indicates a disease involving tissue damage or inflammation. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How long does the absolute refractory period typically last?","choices":["About 180 milliseconds","About 18 milliseconds","About 80 milliseconds","About 360 milliseconds"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It runs from phase 0 to the middle of phase 3, and throughout that time the cell will not respond to another stimulus. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 63-year-old's report lists non-HDL-C (non-high-density lipoprotein cholesterol). How is it derived?","choices":["Total cholesterol minus triglycerides","LDL-C minus HDL-C","Total cholesterol minus HDL-C","HDL-C plus triglycerides divided by 5"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It captures the cholesterol in all atherogenic apoB (apolipoprotein B) lipoproteins, costs nothing extra, and is a better predictor of risk than LDL-C (low-density lipoprotein cholesterol). — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"For which of these is a transesophageal study specifically useful?","choices":["Coronary artery calcium scoring","Assessing exercise capacity","Valve vegetations","Measuring the cardiothoracic ratio"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. All are small, posterior, or both, which is precisely where the transthoracic view falls short. Prosthetic valve function, cardiac tumors and left atrial appendage thrombus. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A patient with an acute myocardial infarction needs the coronary arteries imaged and treated in the same procedure. Which study is appropriate?","choices":["Coronary angiography","Coronary computed tomography angiography","Radionuclide perfusion imaging","Cardiovascular magnetic resonance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute infarction is a listed indication, and balloon angioplasty or stent placement can be done during the same catheter procedure. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman's 10-year cardiovascular risk is estimated with the Reynolds Risk Score. Which biomarker does that score include?","choices":["High-sensitivity cardiac troponin","High-sensitivity C-reactive protein","B-type natriuretic peptide","Creatine kinase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The score estimates 10-year cardiovascular risk in women over 45 from age, systolic blood pressure, diabetes, smoking, high-density and total cholesterol, hs-CRP (high-sensitivity C-reactive protein) and a parent with myocardial infarction before 60. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has thrombocytopenia. Which platelet index describes the uniformity of platelet size and helps with the differential diagnosis?","choices":["Mean corpuscular volume","Mean platelet volume","Red cell distribution width","Bleeding time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mean platelet volume indicates the uniformity of size of the platelet population and is used for the differential diagnosis of thrombocytopenia. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has a risk assessment for arterial thrombosis. Which two laboratory results are independent risk factors?","choices":["Bleeding time and platelet count","Mixing study and reptilase time","Prothrombin time and D-dimer","Mean platelet volume and fibrin monomers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The prothrombin time and D-dimer are independent risk factors for arterial thrombosis. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A cardiac cell is at minus ninety millivolts and able to respond to an impulse. Which phase is it in?","choices":["Phase 0, the upstroke","Phase 2, the plateau","Phase 4, the resting state","Phase 3, rapid repolarization"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and this is the phase in which the cell is ready and can respond, which is what distinguishes it from the refractory portion of the cycle. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What two things does the atrioventricular node do to the impulse passing through it?","choices":["It speeds the impulse up and attenuates it","It blocks every second impulse by design","It introduces a short pause and amplifies the signal","It reverses the direction of the impulse"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pause is what lets the atria finish emptying before the ventricles contract, so a delay in the conduction system is doing useful mechanical work. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 44-year-old with atypical chest pain has a measurable high-sensitivity troponin that is below the 99th percentile limit. What pitfall of the assay does this illustrate?","choices":["It cannot detect small infarctions","It cross-reacts with creatine kinase","It is falsely low with obesity","It detects troponin in healthy people"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The sensitivity is so high that measurable troponin turns up in healthy people, so the entire clinical picture has to be considered. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What limits the use of the three hundred method?","choices":["It only works on a six-second strip","It only works if the rhythm is regular","It only works in lead II","It only works at rates above one hundred"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is why regularity is assessed before rate: the answer decides which method may be used. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 50-year-old's lipid report mentions lipoproteins. What makes up a lipoprotein?","choices":["A protein core wrapped in a triglyceride shell","Free fatty acids bound to albumin alone","Cholesterol crystals coated with fibrin","A lipid core, phospholipid shell and apolipoproteins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The core holds cholesterol esters and triglycerides; the shell is phospholipid; the apolipoproteins are the structural and regulatory proteins on its surface. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man has a negative fibrin monomer test. What does this result mean?","choices":["Intravascular coagulation is excluded","Plasmin activity is absent","The fibrinogen level is normal","Intravascular coagulation is not excluded"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A negative fibrin monomer test does not mean intravascular coagulation is absent; a positive one indicates thrombin activity. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has blood drawn at two hospitals, each with a different reagent. Why is the international normalized ratio reported?","choices":["It measures platelet function","It measures fibrin breakdown","It replaces the partial thromboplastin time","It standardizes the prothrombin time"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Across laboratories, the international normalized ratio adjusts the prothrombin time ratio so results can be standardized from laboratory to laboratory. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A healthy 30-year-old asks why a lipid profile is being ordered at his first visit. Which is an indication?","choices":["Ruling out an acute infarction","Triage of acute dyspnea","Baseline and risk estimation","Screening for muscle injury"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A lipid profile is indicated for baseline documentation, atherosclerotic risk estimation, and guiding and monitoring lipid-lowering therapy in nearly all adults. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What does depolarization represent, and what is its relationship to contraction?","choices":["Recovery of cardiac tissue, and it follows contraction","Activation of cardiac tissue, and it precedes contraction","Activation of cardiac tissue, occurring simultaneously with contraction","The resting state of the cell between beats"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the order matters: the electrical event comes first, so an electrocardiogram showing depolarization does not prove the muscle actually contracted. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 47-year-old's panel shows triglycerides of 480 mg/dL (desirable below 150 mg/dL). What is true of a Friedewald LDL-C (low-density lipoprotein cholesterol) on this sample?","choices":["It cannot be used","It is the most accurate estimate","It is falsely high but usable","It becomes a direct measurement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The Friedewald equation cannot be used when triglycerides are 400 mg/dL or above. The Martin/Hopkins and Sampson/NIH (National Institutes of Health) equations are now preferred. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Why are cardiac medications given before a cardiac computed tomography study?","choices":["To dilate the coronary arteries so contrast reaches the myocardium","To prevent an allergic reaction to the contrast","To provoke ischemia during the scan","To control heart rate, since the moving heart degrades the images"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the study is also synchronized to an electrocardiogram so the heart can be examined at chosen points in the cycle. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"How are veins and arteries told apart on a vascular ultrasound?","choices":["Veins pulsate and arteries collapse when compressed","Veins collapse when compressed and arteries pulsate","Veins appear bright and arteries appear dark","Only Doppler can distinguish them"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Compression is the maneuver the whole deep vein thrombosis study rests on, because a vein that will not collapse has something in it. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 69-year-old with new exertional dyspnea has a B-type natriuretic peptide below the assay's heart failure cutoff. How is that result used?","choices":["To rule in acute myocardial infarction","To rule out new-onset heart failure","To confirm heart failure on its own","To estimate long-term atherosclerotic risk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The natriuretic peptides can serve as a rule-out test for suspected new-onset heart failure, used together with the clinical assessment and an echocardiogram if indicated. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man on heparin has a prolonged thrombin time. Which test stays normal, pointing to heparin rather than a fibrinogen defect?","choices":["Reptilase time","Activated partial thromboplastin time","D-dimer","Mean platelet volume"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The reptilase time is normal with heparin because it mainly assesses fibrinogen function, separating heparin effect from a fibrinogen problem. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has thrombocytopenia, and her smear shows fragmented red blood cells. What are these fragments called?","choices":["Schistocytes","Target cells","Spherocytes","Teardrop cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The smear in thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura shows red cell fragments, called schistocytes, in addition to the thrombocytopenia. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is distinctive about the bundle of His in a normal heart?","choices":["It is one of several pathways crossing the atrioventricular junction","It paces the heart at sixty to a hundred beats per minute","It lies within the atrial wall","It is the only electrical pathway crossing from the atria to the ventricles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Everything above and below has to go through it, which is why a block there separates the two halves of the heart electrically. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which property refers to a cardiac cell's ability to produce its own impulse?","choices":["Automaticity","Excitability","Conductivity","Contractility"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is why the heart beats without instruction from anywhere else, and why several parts of it can take over pacemaking if the one above fails. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 3"}]};
